{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>With a stone or with a fist.</b> In the language of our Sages, a fist (אֶגְרוֹף) is the hand when the fingers are gathered into the palm to strike with it, as they say: 'men of fists' (ba'alei egrofin) (Kiddushin 76a), 'the fist of the watermelon vendor' (Kelim 17:12), and likewise 'a wicked fist' (be'egrof resha) (Isaiah 58:4) — the hand of a wicked person who strikes. And Scripture mentioned two kinds of blows: one severe, which is the stone, and one light, which is the fist, which does not kill in most cases — to say that with both of them an assessment (omed) is required and they imprison him. And if he dies — for this is one who struck him in a life-threatening place — he is a murderer and is put to death, and if he does not die, he pays for loss of income and healing.\n\nAnd in the Mekhilta (here), Rabbi Natan says: Scripture juxtaposes stone to fist and fist to stone — just as the stone must be of a size capable of killing, so too the fist must be capable of killing; and just as the fist is in a [place] that is known [to be life-threatening], so too the stone is in a [place] that is known. This means: if [the stone] was mixed among other stones, he is exempt — to say that if it was mixed among other stones, they assess the lightest among them, and if it is not capable of killing, they exempt him. And others said (Ibn Ezra and Radak here) that אֶגְרוֹף is a clod of earth, from the root of 'seeds have shriveled under their hoes (מַגְרְפוֹתֵיהֶם)' (Joel 1:17), and the aleph is like the aleph of 'arm' (אֶזְרוֹע, Jeremiah 32:21). And this is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 21:18), who said 'with a clod' (בְּכוּרְמֵיזָא). And Scripture mentioned these too, to say that even with a clod one may be executed if he struck him in a place where it is capable of killing. And this is to distinguish the sword from them, for it does not require an assessment, as the Sages mentioned (Sanhedrin 76a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "I read 'וּבַמְּכִילְתָּא' as referring to the Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael on this verse, as indicated by the parenthetical '(כאן)' = '(here)'. The phrase 'בְּיָדוּעַ' in Rabbi Natan's midrash I render as 'in a place that is known [to be life-threatening]' following the talmudic context (Sanhedrin 78a)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:21.556664737Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}